    {key:'displacement',name:'Displacement',full:'Directional body expansion with context',definition:'For these examples, displacement has a real body at least twice the median of the preceding five real bodies, closes in the outer quarter of its range in its direction, and closes through the marked structural level.',rule:'Expanded body + close near extreme + structure break',caution:'The threshold is a training convention. A long wick or a large range alone is insufficient.'},
    {key:'fvg',name:'FVG',full:'Fair value gap',definition:'In three consecutive candles, a bullish FVG is between the first candle’s high and the third candle’s low when those prices do not overlap. A bearish FVG is between the third candle’s high and the first candle’s low.',rule:'Bull: H[first] < L[third] · Bear: L[first] > H[third]',caution:'Use wicks for the boundaries. Equal prices or overlapping wicks create no gap. FVGs need not fill.'},
    {key:'entry',name:'Entries',full:'A conditional plan, with invalidation',definition:'The lab’s entry exercises use a sweep, a displaced structure shift, a zone retest and a rejection trigger. A potential entry remains conditional until its trigger appears. Define structural invalidation and assess a target before any hypothetical trade.',rule:'Context → zone → trigger → invalidation → target',caution:'The exercises teach chart reading, not expected returns. Costs, slippage and position sizing are outside these synthetic snapshots.'}
